From ba0276030ee3b6dcf3e733fb64ae191a35ad6b88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Sesio <aasesio@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:35:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] gbm/drm: emulate HDMI frame packing on connectors without
 stereo modes

The kernel only flags native HDMI connectors stereo_allowed, so a
DisplayPort connector never carries 3D modes, even when its sink is an
HDMI TV behind a DP-to-HDMI protocol converter. That is the HDMI port of
many Intel boards (NUC7/NUC8 and others use a MegaChips or Parade LSPCON
and show up as DP-1), where the frame-packing mode this branch relies on
never appears.

The raster itself is not the problem: scanning out the frame-packing
picture (1080 + 45 + 1080 lines at twice the pixel clock, 1920x2205 @ 24 Hz)
as a plain 2D mode makes an HDMI 1.4a TV switch into frame-packed 3D.
Verified on an Intel NUC8i7BEH (Coffee Lake, LSPCON MC2800) through an
Onkyo receiver into a Panasonic VT65 plasma: both eyes correct, one flip
per vblank at 41.667 ms over several minutes.

So, when a connected connector has no stereo mode at all but the sink's
EDID advertises HDMI 3D (3D_present in the HDMI VSDB), CDRMConnector
appends a frame-packing variant of each mandatory HDMI 1.4a frame-packing
timing it finds (1080p24/23.98, 720p60/59.94, 720p50), marked
DRM_MODE_TYPE_USERDEF. Everything downstream treats them as real
frame-packing modes (iScreenHeight = vdisplay + vtotal, iBlanking,
CRTC_H). Only at commit time, in CDRMAtomic and CDRMLegacy, is the mode
translated to the doubled 2D raster the kernel can accept, using the same
arithmetic as drm_mode_set_crtcinfo(CRTC_STEREO_DOUBLE).

The kernel sends no HDMI Vendor-Specific InfoFrame in this case, so the
TV has to recognise the 3D structure from the timing; the Panasonic does,
other sinks may not. KODI_3D_EMULATE_FRAME_PACKING=0 disables the
emulation.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
